"[2610.23071] Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23071)\nciteturn41302view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23071\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 16 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Aditya Nawal , cite6†Manit Baser , cite7†Mohan Gurusamy L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents, by Aditya Nawal and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large language models increasingly act as agents that call external tools, yet most tool-safety research examines only what follows a tool call. The prior decision of whether to call the tool remains largely unexamined and can influence downstream safety. Across the seven open-weight models, the unweighted model-average harm rate after admitted harmful requests is 74.5%. To identify what governs this decision, we apply a factorial intervention to the input request.\nL17: Within our generated construction, neutralizing the requested-operation component produces the largest admission contrast (19 pp). A linear direction estimated from this contrast influences held-out admission in four of seven models; effects on operation-neutralized requests in some models show that it is not an operation-specific mechanism. We then test whether written refusal is behaviorally and representationally coupled to the same decision. Written refusal accompanies 33.6% of admitted harmful calls.\nL18: Plain-text refusal training reduces unsafe admission in models that retain benign tool use, showing that response-level safety training can transfer to the call decision. Together, these results establish a partial, model-dependent relation between written refusal and tool admission. They motivate direct evaluation and supervision of tool admission rather than treating text refusal as its proxy.\nL19: Comments:  | 19 pages, 5 figures. Preprint version of a manuscript submitted to Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics (TACL)\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2610.23071 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2610.23071v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.23071†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Aditya Nawal [cite13†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Fri, 16 Oct 2026 07:42:47 UTC (582 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Before Retrieval Begins: Safety and Tool-Use Decisions in LLM Agents, by Aditya Nawal and 2 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite8†View PDF L36:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite14†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite15†view license L40: \nL41: ### Additional Features\nL42: \nL43:   * cite16†Audio Summary L44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: cs.CL\nL47: \nL48: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L49: \nL50: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2026-10 L51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: cite22†cs L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL63: \nL64: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL65: \nL66: [Button: ×]\nL67: \