author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:A Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Anglin Cai , cite6†Zhiqi Wang , cite7†Xiaozhi Yang , cite8†Yurun Lin , cite9†Dongwon Lee , cite10†Yuchen Yang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled A Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges, by Anglin Cai and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) increasingly serve as judges in training and evaluation pipelines, often assessing answers together with explanations. Although AI judges are sensitive to textual factors, it remains unclear whether ordinary plausible explanations can sway judgments requiring visual verification. We introduce JUPE, a visual-question-answering (VQA)-style benchmark that fixes answers while varying explanations.\nL17: > Across 14 MLLM judges, ordinary explanations that sound plausible mislead judges, with effects comparable on average to deliberately persuasive arguments. Plain justifications and chain-of-thought rationales reduce rejection of incorrect answers by 12.7 and 13.8 percentage points, respectively. We call this failure plausibility capture. Using signal detection theory, we find a significant mean shift in decision criterion toward acceptance, without a clear change in mean discriminability.\nL18: Explanations sway judgments across domains, explanation generators, and judging protocols, while tested mitigations offer limited, model-dependent protection. Such shifts can inflate performance estimates when judges evaluate answers with explanations.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2610.22631 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or 